// The i386 calls whose registers are already the native ones but whose
// STRUCTURES the handler converts itself.
//
// The other allowlist, `i386_abi_same.in`, is for the calls that need no
// conversion at all: scalars, paths, byte buffers. This one makes a weaker
// claim, and a different one. These calls pass a structure whose i386 image
// is the native one with every word halved -- `struct timespec`,
// `timeval`, `itimerval`, `itimerspec`, `tms`, `rusage` -- and the handler
// writes and reads it at the width the CALLING PROCESS speaks, because it
// goes through `linux_syscall::narrow`. That is Linux's
// `in_compat_syscall()`: the width is asked of the process, not of the call
// site, so one handler serves both ABIs and there is no `compat_sys_*`
// wrapper to keep in step with it.
//
// "Width" covers alignment too, and one entry here is only about that:
// `prlimit64` passes the same sixteen bytes in both ABIs, 4-aligned on i386
// and 8-aligned here, which `UserPtr::write` refuses. See the bottom of this
// file.
//
// So a name belongs here once EVERY structure it passes is converted. A name
// stays out while even one is not, and answers `ENOSYS` until then --
// `pselect6` is the example worth keeping in mind: its timeout is a
// `timespec`, which is converted, but its sixth argument points at a
// `{ const sigset_t *ss; size_t ss_len; }` of two words, 8 bytes to a 32-bit
// caller against 16 natively, and nothing converts that yet.
//
// Not here, each for its own reason:
//
//   - `utime` (30) passes a `struct utimbuf`, which is a pair of words and
//     not an array of these structures -- and whose native number (132) has
//     no handler in this kernel at all, so there is nothing for the table to
//     point it at. The same goes for `futimesat` (299) and its native 261;
//   - `waitid` (284) passes a `siginfo_t`, which is 128 bytes in both ABIs
//     but not the same 128 bytes;
//   - `timer_create` (259) passes a `struct sigevent`, whose union holds a
//     pointer;
//   - `select` (82) is i386's OLD select, which passes its arguments in a
//     block of memory rather than in registers; `_newselect` (142) is the
//     one that takes them the native way, and is the one listed here.
//   - the `*_time64` calls (403 and up) are a separate job: their structures
//     are NOT halved -- that is their whole point -- so they need the native
//     layout with a 32-bit caller, which is a third shape.

// struct timespec, struct timeval
clock_gettime
clock_settime
clock_getres
clock_nanosleep
nanosleep
gettimeofday
settimeofday
sched_rr_get_interval
ppoll
// i386's `_newselect` is x86_64's `select`: the old `select` (82) is the one
// that takes a block of memory, and is deliberately not here.
_newselect = select

// struct itimerval, struct itimerspec
setitimer
getitimer
timer_settime
timer_gettime
timerfd_settime
timerfd_gettime

// struct tms, struct rusage
times
getrusage
wait4

// struct iovec. `preadv` (333) and `pwritev` (334) are NOT here: their
// offset arrives split across two registers, which is a register-level
// difference and so a job for `compat32::wide`, not for this list.
readv
writev

// An ARRAY of two `struct timespec` / `struct timeval`: 32 bytes here and
// **16** to a 32-bit caller, read at the calling process's width by
// `narrow::narrow_get_array`. The stride is the thing that differs, which is
// why this is a helper and not a loop at the call site: reading the native
// array first and halving each entry afterwards reads sixteen bytes past the
// end of the caller's buffer before it converts anything. A null pointer is
// "now" in both, which is `narrow_get_array_if_not_null`.
utimes
utimensat

// struct timespec, again: `futex`'s fourth argument is a timeout for the
// commands that wait and a plain count for the ones that requeue, and the
// timeout is the only thing it points at. Everything else it touches is a
// `u32` futex word or a scalar.
futex

// struct statx: the SAME 256 bytes in both ABIs -- it was designed that way
// -- and still a conversion for the same reason as `prlimit64` below: on
// i386 a `long long` aligns to 4, so a 32-bit program's `struct statx *` is
// 4-aligned and the typed write would insist on 8.
statx

// struct rlimit64: the SAME sixteen bytes in both ABIs, and still a
// conversion, because on i386 a `long long` is aligned to 4 and `UserPtr`
// insists on 8. The handler writes the native layout as bytes
// (`wide_put_unaligned`). The three narrow rlimit calls -- `setrlimit` (75),
// `getrlimit` (76) and `ugetrlimit` (191) -- are NOT here: theirs is a clamp
// and not a halving, so they have their own handlers in `compat32::rlimit`.
prlimit64

// argv and envp: arrays of POINTERS, four bytes each for a 32-bit caller
// against eight natively, read at the calling process's width by
// `Syscall::read_cstring_array`. Not a halved structure like the rest of
// this file, but the same rule and the same reason: reading the narrow array
// at the native width does not fail, it pairs the halves wrongly and the new
// program's arguments come out of addresses the caller never wrote. The
// pointer table's cost against `RLIMIT_STACK` is counted at the same width
// (`exec_args::pointer_size`), because Linux counts the NEW program's
// pointer there.
//
// Everything else these two pass is a path or a scalar, and the image they
// load brings its own ABI: `exec_at` adopts whatever the loader detected, so
// a 32-bit program may exec a 64-bit one and stop being a 32-bit process.
execve
execveat
